## Current code pattern

In `src/main.rs` lines 135-155, `collect_python_files` uses manual mutation:

```rust
fn collect_python_files(path: &str) -> Vec<String> {
    let p = PathBuf::from(path);
    let mut files = Vec::new();
    if p.is_dir() {
        for entry in WalkDir::new(&p)
            .into_iter()
            .filter_map(|e| e.ok())
            .filter(|e| {
                e.path().extension().is_some_and(|ext| ext == "py")
                    && !e.path().to_string_lossy().contains("__pycache__")
            })
        {
            files.push(entry.path().to_string_lossy().to_string());
        }
    } else if p.is_file() && p.extension().is_some_and(|ext| ext == "py") {
        files.push(path.to_string());
    }
    files.sort();
    files.dedup();
    files
}
```

## Estimated savings

~7 lines saved with an iterator-chain style:

```rust
fn collect_python_files(path: &str) -> Vec<String> {
    let p = PathBuf::from(path);
    let mut files: Vec<String> = if p.is_dir() {
        WalkDir::new(&p)
            .into_iter()
            .filter_map(|e| e.ok())
            .filter(|e| {
                e.path().extension().is_some_and(|ext| ext == "py")
                    && !e.path().to_string_lossy().contains("__pycache__")
            })
            .map(|e| e.path().to_string_lossy().to_string())
            .collect()
    } else if p.is_file() && p.extension().is_some_and(|ext| ext == "py") {
        vec![path.to_string()]
    } else {
        Vec::new()
    };
    files.sort();
    files.dedup();
    files
}
```

This eliminates the mutable `files` variable and the manual `push` loop.

I'd like to propose an alternate version of this spec which would make it possible for libraries to implement with no additional code.

> TL;DR - By specifying that libraries are allowed to include additional properties and accept additional arguments, they may make their existing parse methods compatible with the spec which would remove concerns about bundle size.

## Background

Most schema libraries are comprised of some number of factory functions/classes that return an object that include a method that gets called on it to run its logic, this function is called different things [`safeParse`](https://github.com/colinhacks/zod/blob/4641f434f3bb3dab1bb8cb07f44dd2693c72e35e/src/types.ts#L244), [`_validate`](https://github.com/jquense/yup/blob/5a22c16dbba610050e85f123d389ddacaa92a0ad/src/schema.ts#L416), [`_run`](https://github.com/fabian-hiller/valibot/blob/5869f95aec8bf476f46f510188f194c70ddffe1f/library/src/types/schema.ts#L48), [`[testSymbol]`](https://github.com/sindresorhus/ow/blob/2b7fedac1d86d0827a657109c633286890dc8eeb/source/predicates/predicate.ts#L159), etc.

Each library differs fairly significantly in terms of the specific shape of this method, but they are overall fairly close to the `~validate` method that is being proposed in this spec.

This opens up the possibility that, for _some_ libraries, this method could be a superset of this specification with minor code changes, and the cost of implementing this specification would be overall neutral.

## Suggestions

- `~validate()` could accept additional arguments that must be optional
  - This would make it possible for libraries like Valibot that use a additional arguments for things like `config` to reuse their existing methods
- `ValidationError` and `'~validationerror': true` should be replaced with a `ValidationResult<Output>` type that distinguishes between a successful result and a failure result via the truthiness of an `issues` array
  - `issues` may be an empty array to 
  - This allows for distinguishing between success and failure results without `'~validationerror': true` which for the sake of this proposal is pure overhead that libraries would need to add to support this protocol
- `StandardSchema`, `ValidationSuccessResult`, `ValidationFailureResult`, and `ValidationIssue` may all include additional properties as long as they satisfy the base requirements
  - This was not previously forbidden from the spec, but is worth calling out specifically
  - This allows libraries to have their own versions of these types with whatever they want to have instead of having to manually map to exact objects

The resulting spec would look like this:

```ts
interface StandardSchema<Output, Input> {
  '~output': Output;
  '~input': Input;
  // may include additional arguments but they must be optional
  '~validate': (data: Input, ...args: any[]) => ValidationResult<Output>
  // may include additional properties
  // [key: any]: any
}

type ValidationResult<Output> =
  | ValidationSuccessResult<Output>
  | ValidationFailureResult

interface ValidationSuccessResult<Output> {
  value: Output
  issues?: void | null | false
  // may include additional properties
  // [key: any]: any
}

interface ValidationFailureResult {
  value?: any
  issues: ReadonlyArray<ValidationIssue>
  // may include additional properties
  // [key: any]: any
}

interface ValidationIssue {
  message: string
  path?: ReadonlyArray<PropertyKey>;
  // may include additional properties
  // [key: any]: any
}
```

## Implementations

> **Important!** Just because I'm suggesting here that these libraries _could_ implement this protocol this way, does not mean that they should implement it this way. It just means that if their biggest concern is bundle size, they have an option to implement something with minimal additional code.

If we look at the implementation of a number schema in several libraries, they could be modified with minimal changes to support this protocol:

### Zod

Every Zod schema extends from a common `ZodType` with a `safeParse()` method that returns a very similar type as the suggested spec. Adding a `~validate` method that calls `safeParse()` and adding the necessary properties to `SafeParseSuccess` and `SafeParseError` would make it compatible.

```diff
  export type SafeParseSuccess<Output> = {
    success: true;
    data: Output;
+   value: Output;
    error?: never;
  };
  export type SafeParseError<Input> = {
    success: false;
    error: ZodError<Input>;
    data?: never;
+   issues: ZodIssue[] // Exists in error.issues
  };

  export type SafeParseReturnType<Input, Output> =
    | SafeParseSuccess<Output>
    | SafeParseError<Input>;

  export abstract class ZodType<
    // ...
  > {
    // ...
+   "~validate"(input) {
+     return this.safeParse(input)
+   }
    // ...
  }
```

### Valibot

Renaming `_run` to `~validate` should be enough as its return type `Dataset` already meets all of the requirements of the suggested spec

```diff
  export function number(
    // ...
  ): NumberSchema<ErrorMessage<NumberIssue> | undefined> {
    return {
      // ...
-     _run(dataset, config) {
+     "~validate"(dataset, config = getGlobalConfig()) {
        // ...
      },
    };
  }
```

### Joi

All schema in Joi extend from `Base` with a `validate` method that returns an object with either an `error` or `value`. The `error` contains a `details` array that already meets the requirements of the suggested spec. With the addition of a `~validate()` method and adding `issues` to its `ValidationError` it could implement the spec.

```diff
  internals.Base = class {

+   "~validate"() {
+     return this.validate()
+   }
```
```diff
  exports.entry = function (value, schema, prefs) {

    // ...

    if (result.error) {
        outcome.error = result.error;
+       outcome.issues = result.error.details;
    }
```

### Others

I can investigate more libraries if its useful. I believe it will be a similar story for all of them.

## Current code pattern

In `src/cfg/builder.rs`, `build_for` (lines 278-320) and `build_while` (lines 322-359) both have an else-branch handling pattern that is nearly identical:

**build_for (lines 308-317):**
```rust
if !for_stmt.orelse.is_empty() {
    let else_block = self.new_block(BlockKind::Body);
    self.remove_edge(header, exit_block, "loop-exit");
    self.add_edge(header, else_block, "loop-exit");
    let else_end = self.build_stmts(&for_stmt.orelse, else_block, exit);
    if let Some(ee) = else_end {
        self.add_edge(ee, exit_block, "fallthrough");
    }
}
```

**build_while (lines 347-356):**
```rust
if !while_stmt.orelse.is_empty() {
    let else_block = self.new_block(BlockKind::Body);
    self.remove_edge(header, exit_block, "False");
    self.add_edge(header, else_block, "False");
    let else_end = self.build_stmts(&while_stmt.orelse, else_block, exit);
    if let Some(ee) = else_end {
        self.add_edge(ee, exit_block, "fallthrough");
    }
}
```

The only difference is the edge label (`"loop-exit"` vs `"False"`).

## Estimated savings

~8 lines saved by extracting a helper:

```rust
fn build_loop_else(
    &mut self,
    orelse: &[Stmt],
    header: usize,
    exit_block: usize,
    exit: usize,
    edge_label: &'static str,
) {
    if orelse.is_empty() { return; }
    let else_block = self.new_block(BlockKind::Body);
    self.remove_edge(header, exit_block, edge_label);
    self.add_edge(header, else_block, edge_label);
    if let Some(ee) = self.build_stmts(orelse, else_block, exit) {
        self.add_edge(ee, exit_block, "fallthrough");
    }
}
```

Each call site becomes a single line:
```rust
self.build_loop_else(&for_stmt.orelse, header, exit_block, exit, "loop-exit");
```

