## Estimated savings

~12 lines saved with a helper that takes a closure for the body:

```rust
fn write_file_report<T, F>(items: &[T], file_name: impl Fn(&T) -> &str, write_body: F) -> String
where
    F: Fn(&mut String, &T),
{
    let mut out = String::new();
    let show_headers = items.len() > 1;
    for (i, item) in items.iter().enumerate() {
        if i > 0 { out.push('\n'); }
        if show_headers {
            out.push_str("# file: ");
            out.push_str(file_name(item));
            out.push_str("\n\n");
        }
        write_body(&mut out, item);
    }
    out
}
```

## Current code pattern

In `src/cfg/builder.rs`, `build_raise` (lines 558-581) and `build_assert` (lines 594-617) share nearly identical handler-resolution and edge-emission logic:

```rust
// build_raise (lines 558-580):
let handlers: Vec<PendingEdge> = self
    .except_stack.last().cloned().unwrap_or_default()
    .into_iter()
    .map(|target| PendingEdge { target, label: "raise" })
    .collect();
if handlers.is_empty() {
    self.emit_pending_edges(current, &[PendingEdge { target: exit, label: "raise" }], exit);
} else {
    self.emit_pending_edges(current, &handlers, exit);
}

// build_assert (lines 594-616):
let handlers: Vec<PendingEdge> = self
    .except_stack.last().cloned().unwrap_or_default()
    .into_iter()
    .map(|target| PendingEdge { target, label: "assert-fail" })
    .collect();
if handlers.is_empty() {
    self.emit_pending_edges(current, &[PendingEdge { target: exit, label: "assert-fail" }], exit);
} else {
    self.emit_pending_edges(current, &handlers, exit);
}
```

The only difference is the label string.

## Estimated savings

~15 lines saved by extracting a helper:

```rust
fn emit_exception_like_edges(&mut self, from: usize, exit: usize, label: &'static str) {
    let handlers: Vec<PendingEdge> = self
        .except_stack.last().cloned().unwrap_or_default()
        .into_iter()
        .map(|target| PendingEdge { target, label })
        .collect();
    let edges = if handlers.is_empty() {
        vec![PendingEdge { target: exit, label }]
    } else {
        handlers
    };
    self.emit_pending_edges(from, &edges, exit);
}
```

The same pattern also appears in the explicit_exceptions path in `build_stmt` (lines 157-168), with `"exception"` as the label, though it only emits (no fallback to exit). So a slightly more general helper could also serve that case.

## Current code pattern

Throughout `src/cfg/tests.rs` and `tests/fixtures.rs`, the following pattern appears 20+ times:

```rust
let has_X = func
    .blocks
    .iter()
    .flat_map(|b| &b.successors)
    .any(|e| e.label == "some-label");
assert!(has_X);
```

Variations include:
- Counting edges: `.filter(|e| e.label == "exception").count()` (~10 occurrences)
- Counting return edges to exit: `.filter(|e| e.target == exit_id && e.label == "return").count()` (~6 occurrences)
- Checking edge existence: `.any(|e| e.label == "raise")` (~12 occurrences)
- `starts_with` pattern for case edges: `.filter(|e| e.label.starts_with("case "))` (~4 occurrences)

See lines: 199-204, 212-218, 251-256, 270-275, 283-288, 338-343, 354-359, 367-372, 383-388, 410-415, 443-446, 459-464, 472-476, 483-486, and many more.

## Estimated savings

~80-100 lines across both test files. Add helper methods on `FunctionCfg` (or a test trait):

```rust
// In a test helper module:
trait CfgTestExt {
    fn has_edge(&self, label: &str) -> bool;
    fn count_edges(&self, label: &str) -> usize;
    fn count_edges_to(&self, target: usize, label: &str) -> usize;
    fn exit_id(&self) -> usize;
    fn count_return_edges(&self) -> usize;
}

impl CfgTestExt for FunctionCfg {
    fn has_edge(&self, label: &str) -> bool {
        self.blocks.iter().flat_map(|b| &b.successors).any(|e| e.label == label)
    }
    fn count_edges(&self, label: &str) -> usize {
        self.blocks.iter().flat_map(|b| &b.successors).filter(|e| e.label == label).count()
    }
    fn exit_id(&self) -> usize {
        self.blocks.iter().find(|b| b.label == "exit").unwrap().id
    }
    fn count_return_edges(&self) -> usize {
        let exit = self.exit_id();
        self.count_edges_to(exit, "return")
    }
    fn count_edges_to(&self, target: usize, label: &str) -> usize {
        self.blocks.iter().flat_map(|b| &b.successors)
            .filter(|e| e.target == target && e.label == label).count()
    }
}
```

Then 4-line blocks like:
```rust
let has_raise = func.blocks.iter().flat_map(|b| &b.successors).any(|e| e.label == "raise");
assert!(has_raise);
```
Become:
```rust
assert!(func.has_edge("raise"));
```

## Current code pattern

In `src/main.rs` lines 199-222 (QueryMode::Cfg) and 245-265 (QueryMode::Summary), the exact same logic is used to build a `FileCfg`:

```rust
let file_cfg = if let Some(ref func) = func_name {
    match cfg::try_build_cfg_for_function(&source, file, func, &options) {
        Ok(Some(fc)) => fc,
        Ok(None) => {
            log::warn!("Function '{}' not found in {}", func, file);
            continue;
        }
        Err(err) => {
            log::warn!("Skipping {} due to parse errors: {}", file, err);
            continue;
        }
    }
} else {
    match cfg::try_build_cfgs(&source, file, &options) {
        Ok(fc) => fc,
        Err(err) => {
            log::warn!("Skipping {} due to parse errors: {}", file, err);
            continue;
        }
    }
};
```

This 20-line block is copy-pasted verbatim.

## Estimated savings

~20 lines saved by extracting into a helper function like:

```rust
fn build_file_cfg(
    source: &str,
    file: &str,
    func_name: Option<&str>,
    options: &CfgOptions,
) -> Option<FileCfg> {
    if let Some(func) = func_name {
        match cfg::try_build_cfg_for_function(source, file, func, options) {
            Ok(Some(fc)) => Some(fc),
            Ok(None) => { log::warn!("Function '{}' not found in {}", func, file); None }
            Err(err) => { log::warn!("Skipping {} due to parse errors: {}", file, err); None }
        }
    } else {
        match cfg::try_build_cfgs(source, file, options) {
            Ok(fc) => Some(fc),
            Err(err) => { log::warn!("Skipping {} due to parse errors: {}", file, err); None }
        }
    }
}
```

Then the two call sites become:
```rust
let Some(file_cfg) = build_file_cfg(&source, file, func_name.as_deref(), &options) else { continue };
```

## Current code pattern

In `src/cfg/builder.rs` lines 146-172, the `FunctionDef | ClassDef` arm and the default `_` arm share almost the same body:

```rust
Stmt::FunctionDef(_) | Stmt::ClassDef(_) => {
    let line = self.offset_to_line(stmt.range().start());
    let text = self.range_text(stmt.range());
    self.add_stmt(current, line, &text);
    Some(current)
}
_ => {
    let line = self.offset_to_line(stmt.range().start());
    let text = self.range_text(stmt.range());
    self.add_stmt(current, line, &text);
    if self.explicit_exceptions {
        // ... exception edge logic ...
    }
    Some(current)
}
```

The first 3 lines are identical. The only difference is the `FunctionDef/ClassDef` case skips exception edges.

## Estimated savings

~6 lines saved by combining the arms:

```rust
_ => {
    let line = self.offset_to_line(stmt.range().start());
    let text = self.range_text(stmt.range());
    self.add_stmt(current, line, &text);
    let is_def = matches!(stmt, Stmt::FunctionDef(_) | Stmt::ClassDef(_));
    if self.explicit_exceptions && !is_def {
        let handlers: Vec<PendingEdge> = self
            .except_stack.last().cloned().unwrap_or_default()
            .into_iter()
            .map(|target| PendingEdge { target, label: "exception" })
            .collect();
        self.emit_pending_edges(current, &handlers, exit);
    }
    Some(current)
}
```

This eliminates the duplicated line/text/add_stmt triple.

## Current code pattern

In `src/main.rs` lines 287-300, the Format match arm repeats the same structure three times:

```rust
match cli.format {
    Format::Json => {
        let json = writer::write_json_report(&all_cfgs);
        write_output(&mut stdout, &json)?;
    }
    Format::Text => {
        let text = writer::write_text_report(&all_cfgs);
        write_output(&mut stdout, &text)?;
    }
    Format::Dot => {
        let dot = writer::write_dot_report(&all_cfgs);
        write_output(&mut stdout, &dot)?;
